Subject: Mark `long double` with a newtype wrapper

`long double` is the one builtin bindgen renders differently on different
targets without saying so. `float_kind_rust_type` picks by layout size: `f64`
where the type is eight bytes - MSVC, Apple Arm - and `integer_type(layout)`,
i.e. `u128`, where it is sixteen. Both are the right size. Neither is the right
type, and nothing in the output distinguishes either from the type it was
spelled as, so one unchanged header produces `f64` on one target and `u128` on
the next, with a consumer unable to tell that from a header which really said
`double` or `unsigned __int128`.

Whether a consumer can do anything with either depends on the target, and it
cannot tell which it is looking at. Where `long double` is eight bytes it is
`double` under another name, so the `f64` is right about everything except which
C++ type it is - enough to matter to anything which has to name the type again.
Where it is sixteen it is an 80-bit x87 float (x86-64 System V) or an IEEE
binary128 (AArch64 Linux), and Rust has neither type. A consumer which cares has
to treat those cases differently, or refuse both; either way it has to be able
to see what it is looking at.

`--use-long-double-newtype-wrapper` wraps the chosen type in
`__bindgen_marker_LongDouble<T>`, the same shape
`--use-opaque-newtype-wrapper` and `--use-reference-newtype-wrapper` already
use: a fake type the caller declares itself, through `--raw-line` or by
post-processing. What it wraps is left as it was, so a caller who declares the
marker `#[repr(transparent)]` gets the layout bindgen chose, along with the
fact that the layout is all it is.

The wrap is at the `TypeKind::Float` arm of `TryToRustTy for Type`, which
leaves `TypeKind::Complex(FloatKind::LongDouble)` alone: a `_Complex long
double` is already `__BindgenComplex<T>`, which says on its face that it is not
a plain float. Off by default, so no existing output changes.

--- a/codegen/helpers.rs
+++ b/codegen/helpers.rs
@@ -4,7 +4,7 @@
 
